from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2025-june','-q061-120')
def add(n,topic,p,o,c,h,s,t,e):w.add(n,'Mathematics',topic,'Moderate',p,o,c,h,s,t,e)
add(91,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'''D=[−1,1]² and f(x,y)=1+√(y₊), y₊=max(y,0). For y′=f(x,y), y(0)=0, which are true?''',['f is Lipschitz continuous on D.','f is not Lipschitz continuous on D.','The IVP has at least one solution.','The IVP has no solution.'],'BC',['Compare f(0,t) and f(0,0) as t tends down to zero.','Continuity alone gives local existence by Peano’s theorem.'],r'''For t>0, |f(0,t)−f(0,0)|/t=√t/t=1/√t→∞, so no finite Lipschitz constant exists; B holds and A fails. But f is continuous on the rectangle and the initial point is interior. Peano’s existence theorem gives at least one local solution, proving C and refuting D. An explicit construction uses y=x for x≤0 near zero, and for x≥0 the increasing inverse of x=∫₀ʸds/(1+√s)=2√y−2log(1+√y). Both pieces meet with derivative 1 at zero and remain in D on a sufficiently small interval.''',r'''Peano's existence theorem guarantees a local solution to y′=f(x,y) when f is continuous near the initial point. The Picard–Lindelöf theorem adds local Lipschitz continuity in y to guarantee uniqueness, but this stronger hypothesis is sufficient rather than necessary. A square-root nonlinearity is continuous yet fails Lipschitz continuity at zero because its difference quotient grows like the inverse square root. Failure of a Lipschitz condition does not mean that solutions do not exist. An autonomous equation y′=F(y) with positive continuous F can be solved locally by integrating dx/dy=1/F(y) and inverting the strictly increasing resulting function. Domain restrictions on the rectangle control how long the local solution remains valid. Distinguish existence from uniqueness and local from global conclusions.''',[('Does y′=√|y|, y(0)=0 have a solution?','Yes: y≡0 solves it. There are also delayed positive solutions y=(x−a)²/4 for x≥a and zero before a, showing that non-Lipschitz continuity can permit nonuniqueness.'),('Solve y′=1+y, y(0)=0.','Separation or a linear integrating factor gives y=eˣ−1. The right side is Lipschitz in y, so this solution is locally unique.')])
add(92,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'''φ₁,φ₂ solve y″+cos(x)y′+sin(x)y=0 with (φ₁(0),φ₁′(0))=(1,0) and (φ₂(0),φ₂′(0))=(0,1). Which hold?''',['φ₁(x+2π) is also a solution.','φ₂(x+4π) is also a solution.','No constants a,b satisfy φ₂(x+4π)=aφ₁(x)+bφ₂(x).','Some constants a,b satisfy φ₁(x+2π)=aφ₁(x)+bφ₂(x).'],'ABD',['Shifts by integer periods preserve the differential equation.','The two initial-value-normalized solutions form a basis of the two-dimensional solution space.'],r'''Both coefficients have period 2π. Substitution shows a solution shifted by 2π or 4π satisfies the same equation, proving A,B. The Wronskian at zero is 1·1−0·0=1, so φ₁,φ₂ are a fundamental system. Every other solution, including either shifted one, is a constant linear combination of them. Explicitly any solution ψ has ψ=ψ(0)φ₁+ψ′(0)φ₂ by initial-value uniqueness. Hence D holds and C is false. Periodic coefficients do not require the individual solutions themselves to be periodic.''',r'''A homogeneous linear second-order ODE with continuous coefficients has a two-dimensional solution space, and each pair of initial value and derivative determines a unique solution. Two solutions form a fundamental system when their Wronskian is nonzero at some point. Abel's identity then makes the Wronskian nonzero everywhere on the interval. If the equation's coefficients are periodic with period P, shifting a solution by P again gives a solution, because the shifted coefficients coincide. In a fixed fundamental basis this shift is represented by a constant monodromy matrix. The new solution is therefore a linear combination of the old basis, although it need not equal the original solution or be periodic itself. The distinction between periodic coefficients and periodic solutions is central to such questions.''',[('For y″+y=0 with basis cos x,sin x, express cos(x+a).','The addition formula gives cos(x+a)=cos a cos x−sin a sin x, a constant linear combination.'),('Can y″−y=0 have periodic coefficients and nonperiodic solutions?','Yes. Its constant coefficients are periodic with any period, while eˣ and e^{−x} are nonperiodic solutions.')])
add(93,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'''For continuous q on ℝ, consider y″+q(x)y=0. Which existence statements are FALSE?''',['Some q admits both cos x and eˣcos x as solutions.','Some q admits both sin x and cos x as solutions.','Some q admits both eˣsin x and eˣcos(2x) as solutions.','Some q admits both xeˣ and x(x−1)eˣ as solutions.'],'ACD',['At a zero of any solution this equation forces its second derivative to be zero.','A cos x solution forces q=1 by continuity.'],r'''A: cos x solves only with q=1 wherever cos x≠0, hence everywhere by continuity. At zero, eˣcos x has value 1 and second derivative 0, so y″+y=1≠0; impossible. B is true with q≡1. C is impossible already for eˣsin x: at zero its value is 0 but its second derivative is 2, contradicting the ODE. D is impossible already for xeˣ: its value at zero is 0 while its second derivative is 2. Thus the requested false statements are A,C,D.''',r'''For y″+q(x)y=0 with continuous q, every solution is twice continuously differentiable and its second derivative vanishes at every zero of y. This follows immediately from the equation and is a quick compatibility test for proposed solutions. Away from zeros, a nonzero candidate determines q=−y″/y; continuity must extend this expression through zeros without a singularity. Two candidate solutions must therefore give the same q on their nonzero sets and compatible behavior at their zeros. Abel's identity also says their Wronskian is constant because the y′ coefficient is zero. A proposed pair with nonconstant Wronskian cannot be a fundamental pair for such an equation. Check the simplest point or zero before attempting a complicated global coefficient calculation.''',[('Can y=x be a solution of y″+q(x)y=0 on ℝ?','Yes, q≡0 works because y″=0. Away from zero the equation forces q=0, and continuity then fixes q(0)=0.'),('Can y=x² be a solution with q continuous on ℝ?','No. At zero y=0 but y″=2, so the differential equation would require 2=0.')])
add(94,'Partial Differential Equations',r'''For xuₓ+uᵧ=1 with u(0,y)=eʸ, which hold?''',['There is no neighborhood of the origin on which a solution exists.','There is a unique solution on some neighborhood of the origin.','There is a unique solution on some neighborhood of (0,1).','There are infinitely many solutions each on some neighborhood of the origin.'],'A',['On the initial line x=0 the PDE reduces to uᵧ=1.','Differentiate the prescribed trace eʸ along that same line.'],r'''Any classical solution near a segment of x=0 must satisfy uᵧ(0,y)=1 by the PDE. But differentiating u(0,y)=eʸ gives uᵧ(0,y)=eʸ. Equality holds only at y=0, not on any open segment around zero. Every neighborhood of the origin contains points (0,y) with y≠0, so no solution exists there, proving A and refuting B,D. At (0,1) the two values already differ, e≠1, refuting C. The initial line is characteristic, and its assigned data violate the required tangential evolution.''',r'''For a first-order PDE a uₓ+b uᵧ=c, characteristics have tangent vector (a,b). Initial data on a curve determine a standard locally unique Cauchy problem only when that vector is transverse to the curve. If the curve is characteristic, its trace cannot be prescribed arbitrarily; the PDE restricts the derivative of that trace along the curve. On x=0 for the vector field (x,1), the characteristic direction is vertical, so the trace must have derivative one in y. Data agreeing with that derivative at a single point are insufficient for a solution on an open neighborhood, which includes a whole curve segment. Incompatible characteristic data give nonexistence, while compatible data may allow nonuniqueness. Always test tangential compatibility before invoking a Cauchy existence theorem.''',[('For xuₓ+uᵧ=1, is the trace u(0,y)=y+C compatible?','Yes: its y derivative is 1, matching the PDE on the characteristic line. For example u=y+C is a global solution.'),('Find multiple solutions with that compatible trace.','u=y+C+F(xe^{−y}) satisfies xuₓ+uᵧ=1 for any differentiable F with F(0)=0. Thus infinitely many solutions share the trace.')])
add(95,'Partial Differential Equations',r'''u solves uₓₓ+uᵧᵧ+uₓ=0 in x²+y²<1, with boundary values u=1+2x²y² on x²+y²=1. Which hold?''',['The minimum is 1.','The maximum is 3.','The minimum is 2.',r'The maximum is 3/2.'],'AD',['Apply the maximum and minimum principles for an elliptic equation with drift and no zeroth-order term.','On the boundary set s=x², so y²=1−s.'],r'''The operator Δ+∂ₓ is uniformly elliptic, with no zeroth-order term. The maximum and minimum principles put extrema of the continuous solution on the closed disk at the boundary. There x²+y²=1 and x²y²=s(1−s) for s∈[0,1]. Its minimum is zero (at axis endpoints) and maximum is 1/4 (when x²=y²=1/2). Boundary values therefore range from 1 to 1+2·1/4=3/2. They are attained, yielding A,D. The drift term does not alter the boundary-extremum conclusion. Over the open interior alone the strong principle gives strict inequalities, with these boundary values as infimum and supremum.''',r'''A uniformly elliptic linear operator has a positive-definite principal coefficient matrix with a uniform lower eigenvalue bound. The operator Δ+b·∇ has the identity as that matrix and a first-order drift term. With no positive zeroth-order coefficient, its maximum principle bounds a classical solution continuous up to a bounded domain's boundary by its boundary extrema; applying it to −u gives the minimum bound. The strong principle prohibits interior extrema for a nonconstant solution. Boundary optimization is then an ordinary constrained algebra problem. On a unit circle, x²y²≤[(x²+y²)/2]²=1/4 by the arithmetic–geometric mean inequality, and equality is achievable. Specify whether extrema are taken on the closed domain including prescribed boundary values or only the open interior.''',[('If Δu=0 in a disk and boundary values lie between −2 and 5, what follows?','The maximum principle gives −2≤u≤5 in the closed disk. If u is nonconstant, strict bounds hold at interior points relative to the actual boundary extrema.'),('Maximize x²y² when x²+y²=4.','Set a=x²,b=y². With a+b=4, ab≤4, attained at a=b=2. Thus the maximum is 4.')])
add(96,'Numerical Analysis',r'''Forward Euler with fixed 0<h<1 is applied to y′+y=0,y(0)=1. Which hold?''',['The sequence yₙ does not converge.',r'yₙ→0.',r'0≤yₙ≤1 for n≥0.',r'|y(nh)−yₙ|→0 as n→∞.'],'BCD',['Euler gives yₙ₊₁=(1−h)yₙ.','Compare the geometric sequence with exact values e^{−nh}.'],r'''Euler gives yₙ=(1−h)ⁿ with y₀=1. Since 0<1−h<1, it stays between zero and one and tends to zero, proving B,C and refuting A. The exact solution is y(x)=e^{−x}, so |e^{−nh}−(1−h)ⁿ|≤e^{−nh}+(1−h)ⁿ→0. Thus D also holds. This limit is at fixed step size and increasing time; it is not the usual fixed-final-time accuracy limit h→0.''',r'''Forward Euler approximates y′=f(t,y) by yₙ₊₁=yₙ+hf(tₙ,yₙ), with tₙ=nh. For the linear test equation y′=λy, its amplification factor is 1+hλ, and absolute stability requires |1+hλ|<1. With λ=−1 and 0<h<1, the amplification factor is positive and below one, so values decay monotonically without sign alternation. The exact solution is e^{λt}y₀. Long-time error at a fixed stable step can vanish simply because both exact and numerical solutions tend to zero; this does not mean the method is exact or establishes a given convergence order. The standard first-order global error statement instead concerns h→0 on a fixed bounded time interval. Identify which limiting variable the question specifies.''',[('For y′=−y and h=1.5, do Euler iterates converge?','The factor is 1−1.5=−.5, so yₙ=(−.5)ⁿ tends to zero but alternates sign.'),('For the same equation with h=3, test stability.','The factor is −2 with modulus 2>1; nonzero initial data grow in magnitude and the numerical sequence does not converge.')])
add(97,'Numerical Analysis',r'''Suppose ∫₀³f(x)dx=(3/2)[f(α)+f(α+β)] for every polynomial of degree ≤2. Which are true?''',[r'(α,β)=((3−√3)/2,√3) or ((3+√3)/2,−√3).',r'(α,β)=((3−√2)/2,√2) or ((3+√2)/2,−√2).',r'(α,β)=((3−√5)/2,√5) or ((3+√5)/2,−√5).',r'(α,β)=((3−√7)/2,√7) or ((3+√7)/2,−√7).'],'A',['Enforce exactness separately for 1,x,x².','Write the two nodes as r,s; their sum is 3 and squared sum is 6.'],r'''Let r=α and s=α+β. The constant condition is automatic: 3=(3/2)·2. For f=x, 9/2=(3/2)(r+s), so r+s=3. For f=x², 9=(3/2)(r²+s²), so r²+s²=6 and rs=[9−6]/2=3/2. Thus nodes solve t²−3t+3/2=0 and are (3±√3)/2. Either ordering is allowed, giving β=s−r=±√3 and exactly the pairs in A. Linearity shows matching the basis 1,x,x² suffices for every degree-two polynomial.''',r'''A quadrature formula approximates an integral by a weighted sum of values at chosen nodes. Its degree of exactness is the largest degree for which it integrates every polynomial exactly. By linearity, exactness for all polynomials through degree d is equivalent to matching the moments of the basis 1,x,…,xᵈ. For two equal-weight nodes, the first two nonconstant moment equations determine their sum and sum of squares, and hence their product. Solving the associated quadratic gives the nodes, with swapping them leaving the formula unchanged. These are the two-point Gauss–Legendre nodes after scaling an interval from [−1,1]. Do not interpret β as a node: the second node is α+β, so reversing the node order changes the sign of β.''',[('Find equal-weight nodes for ∫₀¹f(x)dx=[f(r)+f(s)]/2 exact through degree 2.','Moments give r+s=1 and r²+s²=2/3, hence rs=1/6. The roots are (1±1/√3)/2.'),('Find nodes for equal-weight quadrature on [−1,1] exact through degree 2.','Weights are 1 each. The first moment gives s=−r; the second gives r²+s²=2/3, so r=±1/√3 and s=∓1/√3.')])
add(98,'Calculus of Variations',r'''For α≥0, J_α[y]=∫₁²(y′)²/x^α dx with y(1)=1,y(2)=2. Which are true?''',[r'(x⁴+14)/15 is an extremal for J₃.',r'(x²+2)/3 is an extremal for J₁.',r'y=x is an extremal for J₀.',r'(x²−x+2)/2 is an extremal for J₁.'],'ABC',['The integrand has no y dependence.','Euler–Lagrange says y′/x^α is constant.'],r'''Euler–Lagrange gives d[2y′/x^α]/dx=0, so y′=Cx^α. Integration and endpoint conditions yield y(x)=1+(x^{α+1}−1)/(2^{α+1}−1). At α=3 this is (x⁴+14)/15; at α=1 it is (x²+2)/3; at α=0 it is x. Thus A,B,C hold. For D, y′=x−1/2 and y′/x=1−1/(2x), not constant, so it violates the Euler–Lagrange equation despite satisfying the endpoint values.''',r'''For a fixed-endpoint variational problem with integrand depending only on x and y′, the Euler–Lagrange equation reduces to conservation of F_y′. In a weighted Dirichlet energy ∫w(x)(y′)²dx with positive continuous weight w, this implies wy′ is constant. The resulting extremal is obtained by integrating 1/w and normalizing to the endpoint displacement. Positivity of the weight also makes the functional strictly convex on the admissible affine space, so the extremal is the unique minimizer. An alternative derivation uses Cauchy–Schwarz: the fixed displacement ∫y′ bounds the energy, with equality when y′ is proportional to 1/w. Satisfying endpoints alone does not identify an extremal; the differential stationarity condition must also hold.''',[('Minimize ∫₁²(y′)²/x² dx with y(1)=0,y(2)=1.','The equation gives y′=Cx², hence y=(x³−1)/7 after normalization to the endpoint difference.'),('Minimize ∫₀¹2(y′)²dx with y(0)=1,y(1)=4.','The constant weight gives y″=0, so the unique minimizer is the affine function y=1+3x.')])
add(99,'Calculus of Variations',r'''Minimize J[y]=∫₀²[a(y′)²+2byy′+cy²]dx, subject to the printed conditions y(0)=10,y(1)=100. Which statements are true: every admissible extremal is a minimizer when''',[r'(a,b,c)=(−2,1,−2).',r'(a,b,c)=(1,0,2).',r'(a,b,c)=(2,−1,1).',r'(a,b,c)=(1,−2,5).'],'BCD',['At an extremal the linear term in the variation vanishes.','Check definiteness of a(h′)²+2bhh′+ch² for perturbations satisfying h(0)=h(1)=0.'],r'''For an admissible stationary y and perturbation h with h(0)=h(1)=0, expansion gives J[y+h]−J[y]=δJ[y](h)+∫₀²[a(h′)²+2bhh′+ch²]dx; the first term is zero by extremality. In B the remaining integrand is (h′)²+2h²>0 for nonzero h. In C it is 2(h′−h/2)²+h²/2, and in D it is (h′−2h)²+h². Both are positive definite, so every admissible extremal is a strict global minimizer. In A it is −2(h′−h/2)²−3h²/2, negative for nonzero h, so any stationary extremal is a maximum, not a minimum. Thus B,C,D hold. The source’s interval [0,2] and interior constraint at x=1 are kept literally; no assumption that y(2) is fixed is needed because stationarity, including any required natural condition, eliminates the first variation.''',r'''A quadratic functional has an exact expansion under y↦y+h: a linear first-variation term plus a quadratic remainder in h and h′. At an admissible extremal the first variation vanishes for every admissible perturbation. If the quadratic remainder is positive for every nonzero perturbation, the extremal is a strict global minimum; if negative, it is a strict maximum. For an integrand a(h′)²+2bhh′+ch², positive definiteness of the symmetric matrix [[a,b],[b,c]] is ensured by a>0 and ac−b²>0. Completing squares verifies this directly. Endpoint or interior constraints determine which perturbations are allowed, and free endpoints introduce natural boundary conditions into stationarity. The positivity argument works regardless of that placement once stationarity holds; treating a mixed term as a fixed boundary constant requires that both corresponding endpoint values actually be fixed.''',[('At a stationary point of ∫[(y′)²−2yy′+2y²]dx, classify it using variations.','The quadratic remainder is (h′−h)²+h², strictly positive for a nonzero perturbation. Hence the stationary point is a strict global minimum.'),('Classify a stationary point of −∫[(y′)²+y²]dx.','The remainder is −∫[(h′)²+h²], negative for every nonzero h. Thus the stationary point is a strict maximum and not a minimum.')])
add(100,'Integral Equations',r'''u solves u(x)=x²+4∫₀ˣ(t−x)²u(t)dt. Which hold?''',[r'u(0)=0.',r'u(2π/√3)=(1/6)(e^{4π/√3}−e^{−2π/√3}).',r'u(π/(2√3))=(1/6)(e^{π/√3}−√3e^{−π/(2√3)}).',r'u(π/(2√3))=(1/6)(e^{π/√3}+√3e^{−π/√3}).'],'ABC',['Differentiate the Volterra equation three times.','The equation becomes u‴=8u, with u(0)=u′(0)=0,u″(0)=2.'],r'''Differentiation gives u′=2x+8∫₀ˣ(x−t)u(t)dt, u″=2+8∫₀ˣu(t)dt, and u‴=8u. The initial data are 0,0,2. The characteristic roots are 2,−1±i√3. Solving for constants gives u(x)=(1/6)[e^{2x}−e^{−x}(cos√3x+√3sin√3x)]. At zero this is 0. At x=2π/√3 the cosine is 1 and sine 0, yielding B. At x=π/(2√3) the cosine is 0 and sine 1, yielding C. D has both an incorrect sign and decay exponent. Thus A,B,C hold; the differentiated equation with its initial data is equivalent to the original integral equation by integrating back three times.''',r'''A Volterra integral equation integrates only up to the free variable x, so differentiating it uses Leibniz's rule and creates endpoint terms. For a polynomial kernel (x−t)ᵐ, successive derivatives lower the degree until the next derivative exposes u(x). The equation becomes a linear ODE of order m+1, with initial derivatives determined at the integral's lower endpoint. Constant-coefficient ODEs are solved through their characteristic polynomial; complex conjugate roots produce real exponential sine and cosine terms. One must retain all initial conditions to make the differentiated problem equivalent to the integral equation, since differentiation alone can lose integration constants. After obtaining a formula, special arguments may simplify the trigonometric factors. Track factors of m! and the original kernel multiplier carefully.''',[('Solve u(x)=1+2∫₀ˣu(t)dt.','Differentiation gives u′=2u and u(0)=1. Thus u=e^{2x}, and substitution verifies the equation.'),('Solve u(x)=x+∫₀ˣ(x−t)u(t)dt.','Two derivatives give u″=u with u(0)=0,u′(0)=1. Therefore u=sinh x; integrating twice recovers the original equation.')])
print(w.path,len(w.packs))
